- You can also specify `ci_method = "mc"` to use **Monte Carlo confidence intervals** instead of bootstrap.
- Set `R` to control the **number of Monte Carlo repetitions** (default = 20000).


``` r
library(wsMed)
result2 <- wsMed(
  data = example_data, #dataset
  M_C1 = c("A1","B1","C1"), # A1/B1/C1 is A/B/C mediator variable in condition 1
  M_C2 = c("A2","B2","C2"), # A2/B2/C2 is A/B/C mediator variable in condition 2
  Y_C1 = "D1", # D1 is outcome variable in condition 1
  Y_C2 = "D2", # D2 is outcome variable in condition 2
  form = "P", # Parallel mediation
  standardized = TRUE, # Compute standardized effects
  ci_method = "mc", # Use Monte Carlo method for missing data
  standardized = TRUE, # Compute standardized effects based on Monte Carlo method
  R = 10000L,   # the number of Monte Carlo repetitions
  alpha =  0.05,  # Significance level
  alphastd = 0.05 # Significance level for standardized
)
print(result2)
```

## For a dataset with missing data
The `wsMed()` function supports three missing data handling strategies via the `Na` argument:

- `"DE"`: Listwise deletion (default).
- `"FIML"`: Full Information Maximum Likelihood.
- `"MI"`: Multiple Imputation using the `mice` package.

**Note**:
- For **`Na = "DE"`** and **`Na = "FIML"`**, the default is **bootstrap confidence intervals**, but you can specify `ci_method = "mc"` to use **Monte Carlo** confidence intervals instead.
- For **`Na = "MI"`**, only **Monte Carlo** confidence intervals are supported. Bootstrap is **not available** when using multiple imputation.



``` r
library(knitr)
data(example_data)
set.seed(123)
example_dataN <- mice::ampute(
  data = example_data,
  prop = 0.1,
)$amp
```

